      "problem": "A teacher gives you a test with a set of fifteen questions, out of which you are told to answer ten. How many ways can you answer ten different questions?",
      "worked_solution": "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $15$ questions, so $n=15.$ We want to select $10$ of those questions to answer, so we have $r=10.$\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to answer $10$ different questions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{15}{C}_{10} & =\\frac{15!}{(15-10)!\\,10!} \\\\  & =\\frac{15!}{5!10!} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot 14\\cdot 13\\cdot 12\\cdot 11\\cdot \\cancel{10!}}{5!\\cancel{10!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot 14\\cdot 13\\cdot 12\\cdot 11}{5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{360\\,360}{120} \\\\  & =3\\,003.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
